\description{
Optimises the electric power of a heat pump's compressor that charges one
thermal tank, which the heat demand discharges, with an optional backup
next to it: a resistive heater or a gas boiler. It mirrors
\code{\link[=add_battery_optimization]{add_battery_optimization()}}: the same \code{opt_data} conventions, objectives
(except \code{"capacity"}), optimization windows and solvers, with the tank in
place of the battery. The compressor's
heat is \code{cop * P}, so there is no conversion efficiency: the COP is the
conversion. The backup heater turns \code{B} kW of electricity into \code{B} kW of heat.
The gas backup (a hybrid heat pump's boiler) adds \code{H} kW of heat that is not
electricity. A space-heating tank may have the house it heats behind it, as
a second, lossy store (see \emph{The house behind the tank}). One compressor may
serve several stores, such as a hot-water cylinder and a heating buffer
(see \emph{Stores behind one compressor}).
}
\details{
Per slot of \code{dt} hours, with \code{S} the level in kWh thermal above the inlet
water temperature (level 0, \code{SOC = 0}) and \code{k = (1 - loss/100)^dt}:
\itemize{
\item \code{S[t] = k * S[t-1] + (cop[t] * P[t] + B[t] + H[t] - heat_demand[t]) * dt}
\item \verb{Tcap * SOCmin/100 <= S[t] <= Tcap * SOCmax/100}
\item \verb{0 <= P[t] <= Pc}, and \code{P[t] = 0} where \code{cop[t] = 0}
\item \verb{0 <= B[t] <= Pb}, also where \code{cop[t] = 0}
\item \verb{0 <= H[t] <= Pg}, also where \code{cop[t] = 0}
\item every window ends at least as full as \code{Tcap * SOCini/100}, the reference
level (or as full as its top allows).
}

\code{B} (the backup heater) is a variable only with \code{Pb > 0}, and \code{H} (the gas
backup) only with \code{Pg > 0}; a tank has one backup at most. The comfort floor
\code{SOCmin} is \strong{soft}: when the compressor and the backup cannot hold
it, the tank keeps giving heat below it, cooling down to the inlet
temperature (level 0) at most, as a real tank would. The \strong{unmet heat} is
the heat that would have been required to keep the tank at least at its
comfort floor: the heat it gives from below the floor, plus the demand it
cannot serve at all once it is at the inlet temperature. It is positive
while the level falls below the floor and 0 while it recovers, so it is a
missing energy, not a measure of how long or how far the tank stays below
its floor. Every window therefore has a schedule.

The run starts from \code{Tcap * SOCini/100}, and the level carries from one
window to the next. The windows are those of the other optimization
functions (one every \code{window_days} from \code{window_start_hour}), plus the
partial windows the run cuts at both ends: when the first slot is not a
window start, the slots up to the first window start form a \strong{leading
window}, and the slots after the last full window a \strong{trailing window},
both optimised like any other window (the leading one from \code{SOCini}, both
with the end-of-window rule at their last slot). Where a window cannot
meet a grid capacity, the tank may end it below the reference level, at a
price above any compressor heat and below the capacity's, and the next
windows fill it back. Only the first
\code{flex_window_hours} of every window are optimised, so with
\code{flex_window_hours = 24 * window_days} every slot is optimised. The slots
past \code{flex_window_hours} run the \code{flexible} schedule, without backup.
}
\section{The house behind the tank}{

With \code{Hcap} and \code{Hloss}, the building the tank heats is a second store
behind it, a "virtual RC battery": \code{R} is the heat its interior holds above
its setpoint (kWh, 0 at the setpoint, \code{Hcap} at the top of its comfort
band), \code{F >= 0} the heat the tank passes to it (kW), and the heat demand
is the house's. Per slot, with \code{kh = (1 - Hloss/100)^dt}:
\itemize{
\item \code{S[t] = k * S[t-1] + (cop[t] * P[t] + B[t] + H[t] - F[t]) * dt}
\item \code{R[t] = kh * R[t-1] + (F[t] - heat_demand[t]) * dt}
\item \verb{0 <= R[t] <= min(Hcap, D[t])}, both hard, where \code{D[t]} is the heat
demand of the \code{house_horizon_hours} after slot \code{t}, \code{(heat_demand[t+1] + ... ) * dt}, over the whole run (past the window, and fewer slots near
the run's end). Outside the heating season the house holds no heat, even
at a negative price. A level within it can always stay within it: with
nothing heating the house, the demand draws the level down at least as
fast as \code{D} falls. \code{F} has no cap of its own.
\item the house has no end condition: every window ends with the tank at least
at its reference level as without a house, and the heat left in the
house does not count towards it.
}

The tank's band, its soft floor, its end condition, the backups and their
order are those without a house. The house starts the run at its setpoint
(\code{R = 0}) and its level carries from one window to the next like the
tank's, so a window uses the heat the last one left there, but no window
has to put it back. The schedule heats the house ahead when that costs
less than the heat it loses before the demand needs it, within the window:
the heat it holds at the window's end is worth nothing to that window.
The demand the tank cannot pass to the house is unmet heat, as without a
house. The slots that run \code{flexible} never heat the house: it serves the
demand while it is above its setpoint, and the tank gives the rest.
}

\section{A store that holds nothing}{

\code{Tcap = 0} is a heat pump without a tank (or a store without a buffer): its
level is 0 in every slot and \code{SOCmin}, \code{SOCmax}, \code{SOCini} and \code{loss} have
nothing to act on. Its heat in equals its heat out in every slot. Without
a house the compressor follows the demand, \code{cop * P + B + H = heat_demand - U}, up to what \code{Pc} delivers, and the rest is unmet heat \code{U}. With a house
it heats the house directly, \code{cop * P + B + H = F}, which is how a heat
pump without a buffer pre-heats a building; the house keeps its floor at
its setpoint, and what it cannot get is unmet heat. Its 2-decimal power
keeps the level within half a rounding step of heat of 0 (\code{0.005 * cop * dt} kWh, above or below), as a tank keeps it at its floor, so the rounding
leaves no heat unmet.
}

\section{Stores behind one compressor}{

With \code{stores}, a character vector of store names (each matching
\verb{^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$}; flextools gives them no meaning), the compressor
serves one store per name, through a valve that serves one at a time and
shares its time within the slot. Each store has its own balance as above
(its heat demand, COP, band, loss, backup, house and unmet heat), and the
stores share the compressor:
\itemize{
\item \code{S_s[t] = k_s * S_s[t-1] + (cop_s[t] * P_s[t] + B_s[t] + H_s[t] - heat_demand_s[t]) * dt} for every store \code{s}
\item \verb{sum_s P_s[t] <= Pc}, the one compressor's nameplate; the backups are
not part of it.
}

\code{opt_data} then carries \verb{heat_demand_<store>}, \verb{cop_<store>} and
\verb{flexible_<store>} for every store, and not \code{heat_demand}, \code{cop} or
\code{flexible}. \verb{flexible_<store>} is that store's static compressor power,
without its backup; the stores' \verb{flexible_<store>} must not sum above
\code{Pc + 0.01} in any slot.

\code{Tcap}, \code{SOCmin}, \code{SOCmax}, \code{SOCini}, \code{loss}, \code{Pb}, \code{Pg}, \code{Hcap} and
\code{Hloss} take one value per store, in the order of \code{stores}, or one value
for all of them. \code{NA} in \code{Pb} or \code{Pg} is no such backup on that store, \code{NA}
in both \code{Hcap} and \code{Hloss} no house behind it, and \code{SOCmin}, \code{SOCmax},
\code{SOCini} and \code{loss} may be \code{NA} where \code{Tcap} is 0, and only there. \code{Pc}
stays one number.

The objective sees the heat pump's electric load, every store's compressor
and backup heater, \code{sum_s (P_s + B_s)}; \code{lambda} penalises changes of
\verb{sum_s P_s}. The heat price \code{h} (below) takes the lowest positive COP of
every store, and \code{Pc + sum(Pb)} in its \code{"grid"} form. Windows, the levels
they carry, \code{on_failure} and the slots that run \code{flexible} work per store;
in a slot that runs \code{flexible}, each store runs its own \verb{flexible_<store>}
and stops at its own top. One store in this form gives the results of the
form without \code{stores}.

The unmet heat of every store counts alike: where the compressor cannot
serve every store, the solver may leave either short.
}

\section{Compressor first, backup heater second, unmet heat last}{

The backup heater's electricity is part of the load the objective sees, like
the compressor's. To use it only after the compressor, and to cool the tank
below its floor only after both, the objective prices them from the
window's \strong{heat price} \code{h}: the highest marginal value a kW of
electric load can take in the window's objective, divided by the lowest
positive COP when it is below 1, so that no kWh of compressor heat is worth
more than \code{h}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"cost"}: the highest absolute import or export price of the window
(EUR/kWh);
\item \code{"grid"}: the largest derivative of the squared net flow,
\code{2 * (max|static - production| + Pc + Pb)} (kW);
\item a weight \code{w}: \code{w} times the grid value (scaled as the grid term of the
objective is) plus \code{1 - w} times the cost value;
\item with \code{lambda > 0}, plus the largest derivative of the ramping penalty,
\code{4 * lambda * Pc}.
}

The backup heater pays \code{2 * h} per kW on top of its electricity, so it only
runs for heat the compressor cannot deliver (it never absorbs a PV surplus
the compressor could).

The gas backup pays the same \code{2 * h} per kW of heat, at the same rank: only
for heat the compressor cannot deliver, before a missed grid capacity and
before the comfort floor. Its heat is not electricity: it is not part of the
net flow that any objective sees, nor of the grid capacities and their
slack, and \code{h} does not count it. The gas it burns is not priced (its cost
is not weighed against electricity), so it never runs where the compressor
could heat instead within the grid capacities, however expensive the
electricity.

When a window has no schedule that holds the band within the grid
capacities, it is solved again with a soft floor: the level may go down to
0, and every kWh below \code{SOCmin} costs \code{20 * h / dt} per slot, more than
any kWh of heat the compressor, the backup heater or a missed grid capacity
(below) can deliver. So the tank only cools below its floor when they
cannot hold it, and is brought back above it as soon as they can. The end
condition becomes soft at the same price, so a drained tank does not have
to be refilled by the end of the window. Without a backup heater and with a
band the compressor can hold, the problem, and so the result, is the one
without any of this.
}

\section{Soft grid capacities}{

As for \code{\link[=add_battery_optimization]{add_battery_optimization()}}, the grid capacities are soft: a window
that has no schedule holding the band within them is solved with the soft
comfort floor and with a penalised slack on each capacity. The slack is bounded by
how far the site already misses the capacity without optimisation (see
\code{optimization_slack_ceiling()}): for import, with the heat pump running
\code{flexible}; for export, with the heat pump off, since a full tank cannot
absorb a surplus. It costs \code{10 * h} per kW (electric), between the backup
and the comfort floor: the solver uses the compressor and the backup to
their limits before it misses a capacity, and misses an import capacity
before it lets the tank cool below its floor. The gas backup is not bound
by the capacities at all. The heat pump off with the tank cooling always
fits these bounds, so \strong{every window has a schedule}.
A capacity left missed is reported with a message; compare the net flow
\code{static + P + attr(, "backup_kw") - production} with the capacities to find
where.

The slots that run \code{flexible} (past \code{flex_window_hours}, or in a window the
solver failed on, reported with a message, unless \code{on_failure = "error"}
stops the run there instead) keep the tank below its top: the
compressor stops as a thermostat would, its power there being \code{flexible}
reduced to what keeps the level at \code{SOCmax}. Below the floor they cool the
tank down to the inlet temperature at most, with unmet heat as above. So the
level stays between 0 and \code{SOCmax} everywhere, and \code{\link[=get_tank_level]{get_tank_level()}}
reproduces it.

Inputs out of range stop with an error naming the argument; nothing is
clamped into range, and no zero profile is returned in their place.
}

\examples{
library(dplyr)
opt_data <- tibble(
  datetime = as.POSIXct("2024-01-01", tz = "UTC") + 3600 * 0:23,
  production = 0,
  heat_demand = c(rep(1, 17), rep(6, 3), rep(1, 4)),
  cop = 3,
  flexible = 1 / 3,
  price_imported = ifelse(0:23 < 6, 0.1, 0.3),
  price_exported = 0
)
P <- add_tank_optimization(
  opt_data, opt_objective = "cost", Tcap = 3, Pc = 1, Pb = 2
)
attr(P, "backup_kw")
attr(P, "unmet_heat_kw")
get_tank_level(
  P, opt_data$heat_demand, opt_data$cop, init = 3,
  backup = attr(P, "backup_kw")
)

}
